\b\f1\insrsid8014045  Differentiate between inflation and deflation.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid8014045 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\insrsid8014045     ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af41\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f41\fs22\insrsid8014045 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\fs22\insrsid8014045 is a situation in which there is a continuous rise in the general price level. 
\par WHEREAS }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af41\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f41\fs22\insrsid8014045 Deflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\fs22\insrsid8014045 is the
\par opposite of inflation and occurs when the general level of prices falls.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\insrsid8014045 Inflation }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8014045 is 
when the money supply increases by more than the real economy does. This means that one unit of currency will buy less than it could in the past. \line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs28 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs28\insrsid8014045 Deflation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid8014045  is when the money supply contracts, making a unit of currency able to buy more than it could in the past. This is the exact opposite of inflation, but it is also bad.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\insrsid8014045 
