00d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b0200000001000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b4c0000002f00770069006b0069002f0047006f007600650072006e006d0065006e0074005f007300700065006e00640069006e0067000000795881f43b1d7f48af2c825dc485276300000000a5ab000000}}
}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid8014045 government spending}}}\sectd \ltrsect\linex0\sectdefaultcl\sftnbj {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid8014045 . If the increase in government spending is financed by a tax increase, 
the tax increase would tend to reduce private consumption. If instead the increase in government spending is not accompanied by a tax increase, government borrowing to finance the increased government spending would increase }{\field{\*\fldinst {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid8014045 HYPERLINK "/wiki/Interest_rates"}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid14549560\charrsid8014045 {\*\datafield 
00d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b0200000001000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b420000002f00770069006b0069002f0049006e007400650072006500730074005f00720061007400650073000000795881f43b1d7f48af2c825dc485276300000000a5ab000000}}}{\fldrslt {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid8014045 interest rates}}}\sectd \ltrsect\linex0\sectdefaultcl\sftnbj {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cf1\insrsid8014045 
, leading to a reduction in private investment, although there is no empirical evidence that supports such interest rate effect on investment}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid8014045 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid8014045 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid8014045 1.      Comments of current account in balance of payment  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\cf1\insrsid8014045         }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af1 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f1\cf1\insrsid8014045 3 marks
\par ANSWER:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af41\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f41\fs22\insrsid8014045 THE CURRENT ACCOUNT
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\fs22\insrsid8014045 The current account balance is essentially the trade balance (exports minus imports), but with net factor
\par receipts from abroad added.
\par If the exchange rate is fixed, then changes in reserves must mirror the combined balance on the current
\par and capital accounts in order to bring the overall BOPs to zero. If the exchange rate is floating, then
\par changes to reserves can remain zero, as the adjustment burden is borne by the exchange rate which
\par appreciates (depreciates) in response to a joint surplus (deficit) on the current and capital accounts.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af39\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f39\fs22\cf5\insrsid8014045 REFRENCE FOR COMPLETE CONCEPT:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af41\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f41\fs22\insrsid8014045 PARTS OF BOP
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af39\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \f39\fs22\insrsid8014045 The BOPs can be divided into three parts:
\par i. Current account,
\par ii. Capital account and
\par iii. Changes to reserves
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qc \fi360\li-360\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx1170\wrapdefault{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin-360\itap0\pararsid7559332 {\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs40 \ltrch\fcs0 
